UNIT isEven
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG isEven(i: any) -> boolean        # throws TypeError or RangeError on rejected inputs

DEPENDS-ON none
# This SIR INLINES the full observable behavior of the real transitive chain:
#   is-even@1.0.0 -> is-odd@0.1.2 -> is-number@3.0.0 -> kind-of@3.2.2 -> is-buffer@1.1.6
# A blind re-emitter MUST reconstruct a STANDALONE isEven with NO require() of any
# dependency. All behavior of the chain is captured below as plain logic.
# There is NO seam (no clock/RNG/io/net); the unit is pure -> ORACLE-CLASS deterministic.
# A THROW is observable behavior: the oracle compares thrown error class + message
# against returned values. Reproduce throws EXACTLY (class AND message string).

BEHAVIOR

# ============================================================================
# TOP LEVEL
#   isEven(i)  ==  !isOdd(i)
# So isEven returns the boolean negation of isOdd(i), and PROPAGATES any throw
# from isOdd unchanged (is-even does not catch). Therefore isEven throws exactly
# when isOdd throws, with the identical error class and message.
# ============================================================================

# isOdd(i) is computed as the following pipeline. isEven returns the logical NOT
# of the boolean it returns; isEven re-throws whatever isOdd throws.

NODE 1  GATE "is it number-like?"  (inlined is-number@3.0.0 over kind-of@3.2.2)
  Let accepted = isNumberLike(i)   # defined in NODE 1a below
  IF NOT accepted:
     THROW TypeError with message exactly: "is-odd expects a number."
     # (this is the FIRST gate; it runs before the integer gate)

  NODE 1a  isNumberLike(i)  -> boolean   # inlined is-number@3.0.0
    # Step A: classify the JS runtime type of i (inlined kind-of@3.2.2):
    #   - 'string'  if typeof i === 'string' OR i instanceof String
    #   - 'number'  if typeof i === 'number' OR i instanceof Number
    #   - everything else (undefined, null, boolean, bigint, function, array,
    #     object, regexp, date, buffer, symbol, Map/Set, typed arrays, ...) is
    #     classified as some OTHER tag (the exact tag is irrelevant here — all
    #     that matters is that it is NEITHER 'string' NOR 'number').
    #   NOTE: kind-of's is-buffer sub-dependency only ever affects the 'buffer'
    #   classification, which (like all non-string/non-number tags) yields false
    #   here. So is-buffer has NO effect on any in-envelope behavior of isEven.
    #
    # Step B: branch on the classified type:
    #   IF type === 'string':
    #        IF i.trim() is the empty string  -> return false
    #        # (empty string, or whitespace-only string, is NOT number-like)
    #        ELSE fall through to Step C
    #   ELSE IF type !== 'number':
    #        return false        # any non-string non-number value is not number-like
    #   ELSE (type === 'number'):
    #        fall through to Step C
    #
    # Step C: return the result of the numeric coercion test:
    #        return (i - i + 1) >= 0
    #   Semantics of (i - i + 1) >= 0:
    #     - For a finite number n:  n - n = 0, +1 = 1, 1 >= 0 -> true.
    #     - For NaN (the number value):  NaN - NaN = NaN, NaN + 1 = NaN,
    #       NaN >= 0 -> FALSE.  So the number NaN is NOT number-like -> rejected.
    #     - For +Infinity / -Infinity (number values): Inf - Inf = NaN -> NaN+1 = NaN,
    #       FALSE.  So Infinity / -Infinity are NOT number-like -> rejected.
    #     - For a numeric-looking string s that survived Step B (non-empty after trim):
    #       arithmetic coerces s to a number via JS ToNumber, twice. If ToNumber(s)
    #       is a FINITE number, (s - s + 1) = 1 >= 0 -> true (string IS number-like).
    #       If ToNumber(s) is NaN (e.g. "abc") -> false.
    #       If ToNumber(s) is +/-Infinity (e.g. the string "Infinity") -> Inf-Inf=NaN
    #       -> false (string "Infinity"/"NaN" are NOT number-like -> rejected at gate).
    #
    #   JS ToNumber(string) rules that matter for which strings coerce to a finite
    #   number (and thus pass this gate):
    #     - Leading/trailing whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) is stripped.
    #     - Decimal integers and floats: "4", "-4", "+4", "2.5", "2.0", "4.00",
    #       "5.", ".5", "-0", "007"  -> finite numbers (PASS gate).
    #     - Scientific notation: "1e2", "3e0"  -> finite (PASS).
    #     - Hex / binary / octal literals: "0x10", "0b10", "0o17"  -> finite (PASS).
    #     - "Infinity" / "-Infinity"  -> +/-Infinity  -> coercion test FALSE
    #       (REJECTED -> TypeError at NODE 1).
    #     - "NaN" / "abc" / any non-numeric text  -> NaN  -> FALSE (REJECTED -> TypeError).
    #     - "" or whitespace-only  -> rejected earlier in Step B (return false -> TypeError).

NODE 2  GATE "is it an integer?"  (inlined is-odd@0.1.2 integer check)
  # Reached only if NODE 1 accepted i (no throw).
  IF Number(i) !== Math.floor(i):
     THROW RangeError with message exactly: "is-odd expects an integer."
  # Number(i): JS ToNumber applied once.
  # Math.floor(i): JS ToNumber applied internally, then floor.
  # For values that passed NODE 1, i is either:
  #   - a finite number, or
  #   - a string whose ToNumber is a finite number.
  # The test rejects (throws RangeError) iff the numeric value has a fractional part:
  #     "2.5" -> 2.5 !== 2 -> RangeError
  #     2.5   -> RangeError ;  -3.5 -> RangeError ;  1.0000001 -> RangeError ;  ".5" (=0.5) -> RangeError
  # The test PASSES (no throw) for integer-valued numbers/strings, INCLUDING those
  # written with a fractional zero or trailing dot:
  #     2, 2.0, -4, 0, -0, "2", "-4", "2.0", "4.00", "5." (=5), "0x10", "1e2", "007".

NODE 3  COMPUTE parity  ->  return !!(~~i & 1)   (this is isOdd's return value)
  # Reached only if both gates passed.
  # ~~i  = JS double-NOT, i.e. ToInt32(i): coerce i to a number (ToNumber), then
  #        truncate toward zero and wrap into a signed 32-bit integer (mod 2^32,
  #        interpreted as signed). For numeric STRINGS this coerces the string first.
  # & 1  = bitwise AND with 1 -> isolates bit 0 (the parity bit).
  # !!   = coerce to boolean: 1 -> true (ODD), 0 -> false (EVEN).
  #
  # KEY INVARIANT (verified against the real package across the 2^31 and 2^32
  # boundaries): bit 0 of an integer is INVARIANT under ToInt32's 32-bit wrap, so
  # this returns the TRUE parity of i for every integer that reached NODE 3,
  # regardless of magnitude (including values > 2^31 and > 2^32, e.g. 3000000001
  # -> odd, 4294967296 -> even). For values >= 2^53 the float already lost its low
  # bits before the function was called, so the value seen is an even integer and
  # parity reflects that already-rounded value (e.g. 9007199254740993 was already
  # 9007199254740992 -> even). isOdd is faithful to ToInt32 parity in all cases.
  #
  # isOdd returns:  true if odd, false if even.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FINAL: isEven returns the negation:
#   isEven(i) = !isOdd(i)
#     -> NODE 3 even  (isOdd false) => isEven returns TRUE
#     -> NODE 3 odd   (isOdd true)  => isEven returns FALSE
#   Throws from NODE 1 (TypeError) and NODE 2 (RangeError) propagate unchanged.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ============================================================================
# OBSERVED-TRUTH TABLE (verified by executing the real is-even@1.0.0 chain).
# Format:  input  =>  RETURN <bool>  |  THROW <Class>: <message>
# ============================================================================
#  --- numbers: integers ---
#   0            => RETURN true
#   1            => RETURN false
#   2            => RETURN true
#   3            => RETURN false
#   4            => RETURN true
#   -2           => RETURN true
#   -3           => RETURN false
#   -4           => RETURN true
#   -0           => RETURN true
#   100          => RETURN true
#   101          => RETURN false
#   2147483648   => RETURN true      # 2^31, even, > Int32 positive range
#   2147483649   => RETURN false     # odd
#   4294967296   => RETURN true      # 2^32, even
#   4294967297   => RETURN false     # 2^32+1, odd
#   3000000000   => RETURN true      # even, > 2^31
#   3000000001   => RETURN false     # odd, > 2^31
#   -3000000000  => RETURN true
#   -3000000001  => RETURN false
#   9007199254740992 => RETURN true  # 2^53, even
#  --- numbers: non-integer / special ---
#   2.5          => THROW RangeError: is-odd expects an integer.
#   2.0          => RETURN true       # 2.0 === 2, integer-valued
#   0.0          => RETURN true
#   -3.5         => THROW RangeError: is-odd expects an integer.
#   1.0000001    => THROW RangeError: is-odd expects an integer.
#   0.5          => THROW RangeError: is-odd expects an integer.
#   NaN          => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   Infinity     => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   -Infinity    => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#  --- numeric strings (OLD is-odd@0.1.2 ACCEPTS and COERCES them) ---
#   "0"          => RETURN true
#   "1"          => RETURN false
#   "2"          => RETURN true
#   "3"          => RETURN false
#   "-4"         => RETURN true
#   "-3"         => RETURN false
#   "+4"         => RETURN true
#   "-0"         => RETURN true
#   " 2 "        => RETURN true       # surrounding whitespace stripped by ToNumber
#   "\t4\n"      => RETURN true       # tab/newline whitespace stripped
#   "007"        => RETURN false      # =7, odd
#   "008"        => RETURN true       # =8, even
#   "2.0"        => RETURN true       # =2, integer-valued -> passes integer gate
#   "4.00"       => RETURN true       # =4
#   "3.00"       => RETURN false      # =3
#   "5."         => RETURN false      # =5, odd
#   ".5"         => THROW RangeError: is-odd expects an integer.   # =0.5
#   "2.5"        => THROW RangeError: is-odd expects an integer.
#   "1e2"        => RETURN true       # =100
#   "1e1"        => RETURN true       # =10
#   "3e0"        => RETURN false      # =3
#   "0x10"       => RETURN true       # =16
#   "0x11"       => RETURN false      # =17
#   "0b10"       => RETURN true       # =2
#   "0o17"       => RETURN false      # =15
#  --- strings that are NOT number-like (rejected at NODE 1) ---
#   ""           => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.   # empty after trim
#   "   "        => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.   # whitespace-only
#   "abc"        => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.   # ToNumber -> NaN
#   "Infinity"   => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.   # ToNumber -> Inf -> coercion test false
#   "NaN"        => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.   # ToNumber -> NaN
#  --- non-string non-number types (rejected at NODE 1) ---
#   true         => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   false        => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   null         => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   undefined    => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   {}           => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   []           => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   [2]          => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   [2,3]        => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   10n (bigint) => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.
#   11n (bigint) => THROW TypeError: is-odd expects a number.

ORACLE value -> oracle.json

SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
    - The two-gate-then-parity pipeline of NODES 1-3 and the final negation.
    - Exact throw classes and messages:
        TypeError("is-odd expects a number.")     -- rejected at number-like gate
        RangeError("is-odd expects an integer.")  -- rejected at integer gate
      (Messages retain the literal "is-odd" text and trailing period, even though
       the public entry point is isEven. Do NOT rename to "is-even".)
    - Gate ORDER: number-like gate (TypeError) strictly precedes integer gate
      (RangeError). A value that is both not-number-like and non-integer yields
      TypeError (the first gate wins).
    - Numeric STRINGS are ACCEPTED and coerced (this OLD is-odd@0.1.2 does NOT
      reject strings — distinct from is-odd@3.x). Empty/whitespace-only strings
      and non-numeric strings throw TypeError; "Infinity"/"NaN" strings throw
      TypeError (coercion test rejects non-finite).
    - Parity via ToInt32 (~~i) & 1; correct for all magnitudes because bit 0 is
      preserved by the 32-bit wrap.
    - The in-scope value set demonstrated above is SELF-CONSISTENT with
      isEven.inputs.mjs: every value the generator can emit (integers incl. >2^31,
      0, negatives; floats; NaN/Infinity; numeric strings incl. whitespace-padded,
      decimal, scientific, hex/bin/octal, leading-zero; empty/ws-only/non-numeric
      strings; booleans, null, plain objects, arrays, bigint) has its mapping
      defined above.

  CARRIED DATA: none. (kind-of@3.2.2 and is-buffer@1.1.6 contribute only type
    classification; no large literal table is required. is-buffer's classification
    never changes any in-envelope result, since 'buffer' — like every non-string,
    non-number tag — is rejected at the number-like gate.)

  OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (NOT emitted by the generator; documented for
  a blind re-emitter but excluded from oracle data because they are not
  oracle-codec round-trippable):
    - Functions and Symbols as the argument: both are classified as neither string
      nor number and would THROW TypeError("is-odd expects a number."), but they do
      not survive JSON.stringify(encode(args)), so they are NOT in genInputs.
    - Boxed primitives `new Number(2)` / `new String("2")`: kind-of classifies
      these as 'number' / 'string' (via `instanceof Number` / `instanceof String`),
      so the real chain ACCEPTS them and returns by value (e.g. new Number(2) ->
      true, new String("2") -> true). These are class instances that do not
      round-trip through the oracle codec, so they are excluded from genInputs and
      noted here only for completeness.
    - No side effects, no ambient reads (no clock/RNG/env/fs/net). No seam exists.

